e.py", "tests/test_screen.py", "tests/test_selection_observers.py", "tests/test_settings_ui.py", "tests/test_tools.py", "tests/workflow_scan.py", "tools/label_preview.py", "tools/mpv_check.py", "tools/power_probe.py", "uv.lock"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/archive/build-plan-slideshow.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-audit-wave-1.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/hdmi-output-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/platform-and-dependency-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/samsung-tv-state-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/moving-the-pi-player-to-a-new-revision.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The login throttle's global ceiling lets a guesser with two addresses hold the owner out, which the F1 decision's rationale says cannot happen", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the ceiling (30 wrong in 10 minutes, all addresses together) applies to every address, including one that has never guessed wrong. One address doubling to 60 s makes about 15 attempts in 10 minutes, so a LAN host with two addresses (IPv4 plus an IPv6 privacy address is enough) reaches the ceiling. Once it does, auth.Throttle.wait_seconds holds every address, the owner's included, and each slot that ages out goes to whoever asks first. Audit-8 F1 ('an attacker locks the owner out') is therefore narrowed from one address to two, not closed. The plan's decision rests on 'the owner's address is never the one being slowed', and the class docstring says 'Per address, so a guesser cannot lock the owner out'; neither is true at the ceiling. The same collapse happens behind the reverse proxy that UI_ALLOWED_HOSTS invites, where request.remote is the proxy for every caller. Remedy by construction: let an address that has signed in successfully within the session lifetime pass the global ceiling, so the bound on many-address guessers stays and the owner's known browser is never held. Pin it with a test: two guessing addresses fill the ceiling, and a third address that signed in earlier still gets in. If the ceiling is kept as it is, put the real trade (a two-address guesser closes the page to everyone) to the owner in the decision; do not reword the docstring alone.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/auth.py", "src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Frame wall's 'writes are held' condition lives in two signals that disagree: the wait is cleared by news, the journal episode only by a write", "recommendation": "Scope: class. It broke because 'is the set somebody else's' is now spread over several fields with no single source of truth. FrameDisplay keeps `_showing_art`, `_wall_retry`, `_not_our_wall`, `_writes_wait` and `_writes_held`, and changes them from `is_ours`, `_not_ours_after_all`, `_hold_the_writes`, `_write_went_through`, `_wall_is_answering`, `_read_art_mode` and `_take_the_sets_news`. The pair this change added already diverges. `_writes_wait.clear()` runs when the set is read showing art (`_read_art_mode`) or announces a mode change (`_wall_is_answering`), but `_writes_held.end()` runs only in `_write_went_through`, after a write succeeds. Here is the path that is reachable: an evening of television holds an upload, so `tv.writes_held` is logged and the episode begins. The set returns to art mode with nothing owed, and the sun curve holds brightness flat overnight, so no write follows. The episode never ends, so `tv.writes_resumed` is never logged. The next evening's hold is not logged either, because `begin()` returns False. An operator reading the journal sees one 'holding the set's uploads' line from days ago and cannot tell whether writes are held now. Fix it by construction, not with another `end()` call: make one owner of the held-writes state whose transitions move both the wait and the episode together (for example, a small object with `held(exc)` and `released()` that every clearing site calls), or end the episode wherever the wait is cleared. Pin it with a test: hold, then return to art mode with nothing owed, then hold again, and expect two `tv.writes_held` events and one `tv.writes_resumed`.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/displays/frame.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "settings_page.host_refused is logged on every refused request, with no episode bound, and anyone who can reach the page sets the rate", "recommendation": "Instance. `server._known_hosts` writes a WARNING for every request whose Host is not one of the page's names. Access logging is off (`AppRunner(access_log=None)`), so this is the one journal line an unauthenticated client can cause as often as it likes: a rebinding page in a browser on the LAN, or anything on the LAN sending a made-up Host. Logins are different. `login_refused`/`password_refused` are held to the throttle's ceiling of 30 in 10 minutes, because a 429 writes no line. observability-strategy.md's rule is 'Episodes, not repetition', and its list of per-occurrence exceptions does not include this event. frame.py states the reason for that rule: 'a line per attempt would bury the ERRORs that are the Player's only failure channel'. The journal is volatile and capped at 256 MB, so a stream of refusals rotates out the evidence of every other failure. Fix: report it once per name per episode (`episodes.ReportOnce` keyed by name, ending when that name stops arriving, or a count over a window). Keep the 421 page as it is. Add a test that sends many refused requests and asserts the journal holds one line per name.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Schedule._verified grows for the life of the process: entries for composed files the tidy removes are never dropped", "recommendation": "Instance. Chunk 04's `_verified: dict[Path, tuple[int, int]]` gains an entry for each composed picture found intact. Only `_intact` drops one, and only when it is called on a path whose stat fails. `_remove_unwanted` unlinks the composed files the feed no longer needs, but leaves their entries, and `_intact` is never asked about those paths again. So the map gains one entry per distinct (master, mat, geometry) the wall has ever composed, across every feed and geometry change, for as long as the Player runs. That is a slow growth in a process the unit now bounds with MemoryMax, and nothing ever releases it. Fix: `self._verified.pop(path, None)` beside the unlink in `_remove_unwanted`, or rebuild the map from `wanted` on each tidy. Add a test that changes the feed and asserts a removed work's path is no longer in the map.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/programmes/schedule.py"], "fid": "R-4"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 4, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "run_label gives the pull the injected clock but not the renderer: the offline hold's monotonic is still time.monotonic, and nothing tests the wiring", "recommendation": "In production this changes nothing, because Clock.system().monotonic is time.monotonic. But `__main__.run_label` builds `LabelRenderer(..., now=clock.now)` without `monotonic=clock.monotonic`, so a caller that injects a Clock controls the pull's stamp throttle and not the renderer's 30-minute hold (Chunk 06's rule). The comment 'One clock for both' holds for `now` only. Commit 3d5f6f8 has no test through run_label. Learnings: 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value'. Passing monotonic=clock.monotonic is a one-line change that can ride the next commit.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/__main__.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A Host or Origin port of non-ASCII digits raises ValueError in the middleware, a 500 rather than a 421", "recommendation": "`settings_ui/server._split_authority` does `int(port_text) if port_text.isdigit()`. `'\u00b2'.isdigit()` is True and `int('\u00b2')` raises, so `Host: 10.0.0.1:\u00b2` ends as an unhandled exception in `_known_hosts` (and in `origin_allowed` for an Origin). It gives no access and costs a traceback in the journal. Use `port_text.isascii() and port_text.isdigit()`, or catch the ValueError as 'no port'.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "CACHE_DIR_NAMES says 'every name this Player keeps directly under CACHE_DIR' but omits .labels and .client.etag", "recommendation": "Chunk 10's `pull.LABEL_CACHE_DIRNAME` ('.labels') and `pull.CLIENT_ETAG_FILENAME` ('.client.etag') also sit under CACHE_DIR and are not in the set. They are harmless today, because PLAIN_COMPONENT refuses a leading dot, and the comment's own reason for listing dot names ('keeps the set true if that rule changes') is the case they would break. The set is a hand-made list of constants, so the next name added under CACHE_DIR has the same gap. Learnings: 'When code is added, moved or retired, ask which guards were scoped to the old shape'. Add the two names, or derive the set from one registry of the CACHE_DIR names. No fix commit is owed for this alone.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/pull.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "A retired label's last call logs label.renderer_started for a label that captions nothing", "recommendation": "The supervisor's once-more retire call runs `run_label` with stop already set. `LabelRenderer.run` logs 'label X on Y captions wall Z' (label.renderer_started) before it blanks the panel, so the journal says a renderer started for a label just taken away. This is a journal wording matter; nothing acts on it.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/label_renderer.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The wording sweep left 'and a the server that is gone' in a test docstring", "recommendation": "tests/test_availability.py's module docstring. It is the only such artefact in the diff (a grep of the added lines for 'a the', 'the the' and 'an the' finds no other). Inert prose: fix it if a commit is happening anyway.", "files": ["tests/test_availability.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Pull.cycle is now a test-only composition that production no longer runs", "recommendation": "Before this change `Pull.run` looped over `cycle`, so about 100 test calls through `cycle` exercised the production path. Now `run` composes `_poll` with a separate `_heartbeats` task on its own clock, and `cycle` (poll, then forward the heartbeat in sequence) has no caller in src/ (client.py's `self.cycle()` is the Supervisor's). The few tests that drive `run` itself cover its scheduling today, but a later change to `run` is invisible to the bulk of the suite. Either drive production through `cycle` (for example, `run` calls `cycle` and the heartbeat task covers only the backoff gap), or rename `cycle` to say it is a test step. No change is wanted this round if the owner accepts the split.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/pull.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Retirement is signalled two ways, plus a modal 'called once more, already stopped' convention every worker must honour", "recommendation": "Wall workers learn retirement from `WallStop.retired`, a flag on an asyncio.Event subclass that `wall_retired()` reads with an isinstance check, so a plain Event silently reads 'not retired'. Label workers learn it from a second `retired` Event. `_keep_running` bridges the two with an `is_retired` callable. Separately, the recorded Chunk 05 decision makes every Worker handle being called with its stop already set, by doing only its leaving and returning. `run_frame_wall` and `run_screen_wall` now check `stop.is_set()` at the top for this reason. A worker added later that lacks that guard does its setup (mkdir, TV connect, a Pull) for a retired wall. A separate `leave` callable per worker, or one retirement representation for both kinds, would take that knowledge out of each worker. The decision is recorded and vetoable, so this is advisory.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/client.py", "src/arrt_player/__main__.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "FakeTv is importable from two modules after the fan-out that needed the re-export has finished", "recommendation": "Chunk 01's re-export from tests/fakes.py served the partition while the chunks fanned out. On the integrated branch, tests/test_samsung_client.py imports FakeTv from `fake_tv` and six other files (conftest, test_availability, test_bindings, test_client, test_frame_wall, test_majors) import it from `fakes`. Once nothing needs the shim, point the importers at `fake_tv` and drop the re-export, or keep it and say so. This is low cost either way.", "files": ["tests/fakes.py", "tests/fake_tv.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two leak scanners overlap in tests/test_repository.py", "recommendation": "The new working-tree scan (`_ADDRESS_SHAPES` over `_working_tree()`) and the older `test_no_source_file_carries_a_deployment_value` (`_PRIVATE_SHAPES` over src/ and tools/ via a `*.py` glob) both check the private-IPv4 and home-path shapes, from two separate file listings (`_tracked` and `_working_tree`). Only the old test also carries `_DEPLOYMENT_PATHS`. Folding `_DEPLOYMENT_PATHS` into the new scan, scoped to code lines, would leave one scanner. `_ACCEPTED_RESIDUE` is an empty mechanism kept with its own test. Advisory only.", "files": ["tests/test_repository.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Small duplications between the settings page and config", "recommendation": "`settings_ui/server.py` defines `ALLOWED_HOSTS_SETTING = \"UI_ALLOWED_HOSTS\"`, while `config.load_ui` spells the key as a literal. `server.name_allowed` repeats the parse that `config._is_ip_address` does, and the two modules each normalise a host (`.rstrip('.').lower()`). One shared constant and helper in config would keep the refusal message and the setting from drifting apart. Advisory only.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py", "src/arrt_player/config.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The wording sweep left two doubled articles", "recommendation": "Each is prose that nothing reads. tests/test_availability.py:1 reads 'and a the server that is gone', and deploy/README.md:679-680 reads 'start the / the server' across the line break. A grep for doubled articles that spans line breaks finds both and finds no others in the tree. This is the case the learnings rule 'After a scripted mass edit, verify with a method keyed on something the edit did not key on' warns about: the sweep was checked by the count of 'plane', which these leftovers do not contain. Fix them in a commit that is already happening; no separate commit is wanted.", "files": ["tests/test_availability.py", "deploy/README.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "tests/workflow_scan.py's docstring still describes one pattern", "recommendation": "The module docstring opens 'The one pattern that says a workflow line runs pytest' but the module now also holds `unpinned_actions`, `unpinned_setup_uv` and `unlocked_uv_calls`. Nothing reads the wording. Make the opening relational (the workflow scans the CI tests share) the next time the file is touched.", "files": ["tests/workflow_scan.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "deploy/README still says the Player reads art mode 'before every selection'", "recommendation": "Under The dependency moves, the README's note says `get_artmode` is asked 'before every selection'. Since Chunk 02, `TvClient._permit` reads it before every write in `WRITES`: upload, remove, brightness and the slideshow switch as well as show. The note is historical in tone and nothing acts on it. Make the claim relational ('before every write the seam declares, `tv.client.WRITES`') if the section is edited again.", "files": ["deploy/README.md"], "oid": "O-13"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Label cache files outlive their labels, and the record that states the cache's lifecycle does not say so", "recommendation": "`LabelPull` writes `CACHE_DIR/.labels/<id>.json`, and nothing removes a file once its label is no longer mapped to this client. The landing log records this as residual, but no artifact does. The harm is small: a stale file's stamp soon ages past the 30-minute hold, and `label.cache_other_wall` refuses one for another wall. data-model.md's 'Nothing removes a wall's directory' paragraph is where a reader looks for the cache's lifecycle, and it names only the wall directories. No edit is wanted unless the paragraph is touched anyway.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", "src/arrt_player/pull.py"], "oid": "O-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: the change fragment says step 7b checks coordinates, and it checks only *_SECONDS", "recommendation": "The consumer note in `.prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md` says a bad `*_SECONDS` value 'or a latitude or longitude out of range' now stops the Player, and that 'the runbook's step 7b greps for it'. Step 7b's grep matches only `*_SECONDS` keys. The damage is limited: runbook step 2 sends the operator to deploy/README.md \u00a7 Two things that can stop an upgrade, which names the `load()` check for any other refused key, and step 13 names the key if the start fails. Clears no bar, so this is not a finding. Making the clause relational would settle it ('step 7b and README's load() check').", "files": [".prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md"], "oid": "O-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: two phrasings in the change fragment are looser than the code, and nothing reads them", "recommendation": "(1) 'The old plane wording is gone from ... the records.' It is gone from src/, the unit, the tools and the rewritten artifacts. It remains in platform-and-dependency-findings.md and samsung-tv-state-findings.md as dated history, and in quotations of Arrt's own wording. (2) The television paragraph lists selection among the writes held 'unless the set is in art mode, or dark'. Selection needs art mode on (`Gate.ART_MODE`). Nothing reads either sentence, and no edit is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md"], "oid": "O-16"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: data-model's .client.etag row under-claims what the C2 fix enforces", "recommendation": "data-model.md's row for `.client.etag` still says the ETag is 'Sent only while `.client.json` exists'. Since Chunk 03, `ClientPull._cached_etag` also requires the document to parse with `parse_client_document`, which the manifest.etag row beside it now says for its own file. Learnings: 'When an index under-claims its enforcement, fix it'. It clears no Records Pass bar, so fold the fix into the next edit of that table.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md"], "oid": "O-17"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: runbook steps now run 7, 7b, 7c, 7a", "recommendation": "7b and 7c were inserted ahead of the existing 7a, so the labels on the page are out of order. An operator who reads top to bottom still does every step, and no step's cross-reference is wrong. No edit is wanted unless the runbook is renumbered for another reason.", "files": [".prawduct/runbooks/moving-the-pi-player-to-a-new-revision.md"], "oid": "O-18"}], "duration_seconds":